📚 Theory Notes
1. input() Function

input() is used to accept data from the user.

Example

name = input("Enter your name: ")

The program waits until the user enters a value and presses Enter.

2. Important Rule

input() always returns a string.

Example

User enters

30

Python stores it as

"30"

not

30

Always convert input to the correct data type when needed.

3. Type Conversion

Convert input to integer

age = int(input("Enter Age: "))

Convert input to float

price = float(input("Enter Price: "))

Convert yes/no input to Boolean

passport_available = input().strip().lower() == "yes"
4. Strings

A string is a sequence of characters.

Example

destination = "Thailand"
5. String Indexing

Each character has an index.

T h a i l a n d
0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7

Example

destination[0]

Output

T

Negative Index

destination[-1]

Output

d
6. String Slicing

Syntax

string[start:end]

Example

destination[1:4]

Output

hai

End index is excluded.

7. Common String Methods
Upper Case
name.upper()
Lower Case
name.lower()
Title Case
name.title()
Remove Spaces
name.strip()
Find Length
len(name)
Replace
city.replace("Goa", "Thailand")
Find
city.find("land")

Returns the starting index if found, otherwise -1.

Starts With
city.startswith("Thai")
Ends With
city.endswith("land")
